<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shechem</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shechem was an ancient city of the central hill country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, at Tell Balata near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>. Probably the Sekmem named on the stele of the Egyptian officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khusobek">Khusobek</a>, it was a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> town and appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> it was an early Israelite centre and the first capital of the northern kingdom of Israel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shechem.jpg" alt="Shechem" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TrickyH · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tell_Balata.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shechem lay in a narrow valley between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ebal">Mount Ebal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a>, two of the largest mountains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>. The valley was a principal highway for merchants and travellers moving between the northern and southern hills, which made the city a trade hub. Many springs made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> easy to dig, and the fertile soil supported agriculture and livestock. The site was identified as Tell Balata after a German party led by Hermann Thiersch came upon it in 1903.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest settlement dates to the Chalcolithic period, around 3500 to 3000 BCE. The first substantial building belongs to the Middle Bronze Age IIA, around 1900 BCE, and the city is mentioned on the stele of Sebek-khu, a noble at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a>. In the Middle Bronze IIC, around 1590 to 1550 BCE, it reached its largest size and power, with a Fortress Temple and cyclopean walls. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> of about 1350 BCE, Šakmu was the centre of a kingdom made by the Canaanite prince Labaya, who recruited Habiru mercenaries.</p><p>In the Iron Age II the city, in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, had an estimated 1,200 inhabitants in the 9th and 8th centuries BCE. In the Hellenistic and Roman periods it was the main settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a>, whose religious centre stood on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a>. Josephus says Sextus Vettulenus Cerialis destroyed it in the First Jewish-Roman War, and in 72 CE Vespasian built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Flavia Neapolis</a> two kilometres to the west, the origin of the name Nablus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> mentions Shechem some sixty times. Genesis tells that Abraham built an altar at the oak of Moreh there, and that Jacob's sons Simeon and Levi killed the city's men after the abduction of their sister Dinah. Joshua assembled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> there to choose whom to serve. Abimelech, son of Gideon, was made king there and later destroyed the city. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s death the northern tribes met at Shechem, and Jeroboam fortified it as the capital of the new kingdom. The kings later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirzah">Tirzah</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, and Shechem lost importance.</p><p>Around 128 BCE the Hasmonean ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a> destroyed the Samaritan temple on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a> and in 107 BCE the city, which never recovered. Jacob's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a> and Joseph's tomb stand nearby, and some scholars identify Shechem with the Samaritan city of Sychar in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shechem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the Books of Kings, an assembly at <b>Shechem</b> asked Solomon’s son Rehoboam to lighten the forced labour and taxes of his father’s reign; when he refused, the northerners made Jeroboam I their king, a former overseer of Solomon’s labour gangs who had taken refuge in Egypt…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the books of Kings, the northern tribes rejected Solomon’s son Rehoboam at <b>Shechem</b> and made Jeroboam their king, founding the Kingdom of Israel, while the tribe of Judah, with much of Benjamin, stayed loyal to Rehoboam and the House of David.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Levant the record is thinner, but the stele of the officer Khusobek mentions a campaign of Senusret III to a place called <b>Sekmem</b>, probably Shechem.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…official in charge of forced labour for the house of Joseph, rebelled and fled to Egypt, where he was protected by the pharaoh Shishak, usually identified with Shoshenq I. When Solomon died around 930 BCE, his son Rehoboam went to <b>Shechem</b> to be acclaimed by the northern tribes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
